// The owner's two feature teardowns (docs/teardowns) against the owner's Full Agent Capability List
// (docs/capability-list/full-agent-capability-list-2026-10-11.md, items 1..149 in document order). Read by
// scripts/build-teardowns.cjs, which gives every feature row its `capability_items` and a derived `upmore_today` (the best
// status among those items, from scripts/capability-list.cjs), and fails --check when this map drifts from the docs.
//
// SECTIONS, one entry per (app, section) that has feature rows, keyed "App/Section":
//   [items, coverage, note]
//   items     the capability-list item numbers the section's features are ([] when the list has no such item)
//   coverage  "full": every feature in the section is one of those items; "part": some are (the note says which part and
//             what is left); "none": the list has no item for it (the note says what the section is)
//   Strict: an item is named only when the features genuinely are that capability. A section about the app's own
//   membership billing, marketing, creator program, bank, card, brokerage or lending is "none" even when Upmore touches
//   the same words. A "part" section is a summary only: every one of its rows is placed in ROWS.
//
// ROWS, keyed by row id: { feature, items, coverage, note }. Two kinds:
//   - the rows of a "full" or "none" section that clearly differ from their section;
//   - every row of a "part" section, placed on its own (the build fails on a row that would be counted PART without
//     its own entry here). Coverage at row level: "full" when the row is wholly those items, "none" with [] when the
//     list has no such item, "part" only when the row itself is half covered, with a note naming the missing half.
// The same kind of feature gets the same placement in every app: linking, syncing and unlinking are 1, a hand-added
// account or holding is 4, finding or totalling transactions (search, filters) is 7, a category fix or a rule that
// remembers it is 8, a split is 11, an automatic market-value feed for a home or car is the missing half of 15, a
// lifetime plan (retirement, Social Security, Monte Carlo) has no item while its life events are what-ifs (20), and
// tags, notes, review queues, bulk edits, transfer matching, exports and the app's own catalog, billing and support
// have no item. `feature` must still match the row (a renumbered row fails the build). Mapping never lifts a gate: a
// hard-gated row stays gated whatever Upmore has.
